# Per-scenario rubric for the LLM judge layer.
#
# Four criteria per step, weights summing to 1.0: map_quality 0.40 / specificity 0.25
# / justification 0.20 / uncertainty 0.15.
#
# CYCLE 1 / ATTEMPT 3 on bagisto, TWO-STEP PROBE
# (plans/cycle-1-craft-the-scenario/01-author.md). TWO steps, names verbatim and in the
# order of scenarios/bagisto.yaml (the judge pairs by name). This is a RE-QUESTION: the
# anchor is unchanged and the question has moved, so this rubric is rewritten to score the
# new axis and not the old one.
#
# WHAT CHANGED, AND WHY THIS RUBRIC CHANGED WITH IT
# Attempt 1 scored WHICH PLACES HOLD a record: measured baseline 0.6471 / sense 0.7647. The
# plain arm's accessor sweep printed most holding sites with their lines attached.
# Attempt 2 scored WHICH KIND-SPECIFIC SLICE a site owns: measured baseline 0.8235 / sense
# 0.8235, a dead tie. The ask named the slices, so one disjunctive grep over their names
# printed the answer set with path:line already on it.
# This attempt scores PROVENANCE: by what ROUTE a record arrived at a site, and at which line
# the handle was established. That is deliberately the one thing an occurrence list cannot
# state - three of the eighteen scored sites carry no occurrence of the record's name on the
# cited line at all, and at several more the name that IS on the line belongs to a
# neighbouring row rather than to the record. A judge that rewards "this file touches the
# record" is scoring attempt 1's question again.
#
# WHY map_quality carries the most weight: coverage and placement are one question. The
# scored set is eighteen sites over eleven areas and they have no shared abstraction - the
# handle is pushed in from outside at one, taken across a neighbour at several, forwarded
# through a neighbour's own method at two, pulled from an untyped bag at one, fabricated
# unsaved under an alias at one, and taken as an undeclared parameter at several. The failure
# this criterion exists to catch is an answer that lists the record's consumers instead of
# tracing how each one obtained a record: the storefront and checkout read path touches these
# records constantly by an ordinary declared accessor, and an answer full of it has answered
# the FIRST ticket in this series rather than this one.
#
# WHY specificity demands the line AND the routine AND the route AND the establishing line:
# a file is not an answer, and here neither is a site. Several scored files hold more than one
# candidate line; three sibling admin controllers are near-identical and differ only in which
# kind they are mounted for; and for most rows the line where the handle was ESTABLISHED is in
# a different file from the line that depends on it (OrderRepository.php:220 is established in
# OrderItem.php:53/:70; Type/Bundle.php:150 is established at Product.php:359). A row that
# pins the site but not the establishing line has given the maintainer half of what the
# narrowing needs, because the narrowing rewrites the establishing line and not the use.
#
# WHY step 1 asks for the write path, the record's self-handoff and the base class explicitly:
# they are the free half, both arms reach them in one lookup, they do not score, and pinning
# them in step 1 puts the discriminator on the far side of them. What step 1 does NOT ask for
# is the enumeration of the far side: measured on the neighbouring repo in this vertical,
# asking step 1 for the enumerated working ground moved BOTH arms together, because the plain
# arm wrote its covering sweep in step 1 and re-read the same output in step 2.
#
# WHY uncertainty is worth its weight here: memorization_probe returns ok:false at recall
# 0.458 on this record's own member surface (33 of 72 members named closed-book, samples
# 0.431 / 0.444 / 0.458). Every one of those members sits in the non-scoring contract group,
# so recitation buys nothing on the discriminator - but it makes confident assertion cheap,
# and on a provenance question the cheapest wrong answer is asserting a route from a
# framework convention (Laravel's relation naming) rather than from the line that establishes
# it. The clone is a single squashed commit, so nothing here can be churn-dated; the judge
# should reward an answer that says which lines it actually opened.
audience: |
  An AI coding agent about to rework the catalogue record this store platform hangs off. The
  change is a narrowing: after it, no routine may end up working on one of these records
  unless it was handed one explicitly. Every place that today obtains one by some other means
  has to be found and rewritten first. Score for that agent, not a human reader and not a docs
  page.
  A perfect answer leaves the agent able to make that change knowing two things. First, what
  the record is today - which of the several same-named things in this repo it actually is,
  where it is declared, what it relates outward to, how it answers for a field that was never
  declared on it, what the one layer whose job is to make and change these records is, and the
  exact line where the record builds the object that speaks for it and hands that object a
  handle back on itself. Second, and this is what decides the answer: every place downstream
  that obtains a record by an indirect route, with the route named and the line where the
  handle was established.
  The distinction the whole ticket turns on is between a record that was HANDED to a routine
  and one the routine got hold of some other way. A declared, typed parameter is the first and
  it is not interesting. The second is a line somewhere else entirely - in the middle of a
  routine about saving an option payload, indexing a price, validating a shipment, rendering
  an admin grid, running an import, deciding whether an order is complete - where the routine
  holds a row of some neighbouring table and steps across to a record from it, or asks that
  neighbour a question the neighbour quietly forwards to the record, or receives a record in
  an argument carrying no declared type anywhere, or takes one out of an untyped bag by string
  key, or builds an unsaved one under an alias, or simply IS a record and refers to itself.
  These sites have no shared abstraction and none of them knows about the others. The set can
  only be assembled by working out, per site, where the handle came from - and for most of
  them that line is in a DIFFERENT FILE from the site itself. At several the record's own name
  does not appear on the depending line at all, and at several others the name that appears
  belongs to the neighbour, so the text on the line actively misdescribes what is held.
  Most of this layer sits in parts of the codebase whose own subject has nothing to do with
  the catalogue, and the parts that do sit near the record are its peripheral write and index
  helpers rather than the record's own file. An answer dense in the record's own file and its
  obvious consumers, and thin everywhere else, has enumerated inside one search scope and
  mistaken it for the codebase.
  Every site must be pinned to the exact file:line with the routine it sits in named, with the
  route by which a record reached it said concretely, with the line where the handle was
  established, and with one phrase on what breaks under the narrowing if nothing is done.

steps:
- name: Map the catalogue record contract
  criteria:
    map_quality:
      weight: 0.4
      question: |
        Is the record presented as one connected picture - which of the several same-named
        things in this repo it actually is, what it relates outward to, how it answers for a
        field that was never declared on it, and how it hands ITSELF to the object that speaks
        for it - rather than as a list of routines or a roll of places the name appears? Two
        of those matter most and they are the two the ticket turns on: that asking the record
        for an undeclared field SUCCEEDS, because a lookup is done behind the scenes rather
        than a property being read; and that the record establishes, from its own side, the
        handle that a whole family of other objects then holds on it - nothing on the
        receiving side takes that handle, it is pushed in. An answer that locates that line,
        names the base class receiving it and says which part of that base's state is the
        record, has understood why the next step is a question about provenance at all.
        The step then asks for the declared write layer at file:line with a phrase on what it
        is responsible for. This part is deliberately the easy part and it is asked for on
        purpose - it is the half where getting hold of a record is written down openly, and
        the next step is about the routes that are not - so score coverage and location here,
        not difficulty.
        The step finally asks for a deliberately SMALL third thing: what it looks like when a
        routine somewhere else has obtained a record with none of that visible, with one
        located example AND the line where that example's handle is actually taken. Score
        whether the shape is characterised correctly. Do NOT reward an exhaustive enumeration
        of those places: it was not asked for, and a long located list is not a better answer
        here than one accurate observation. Could the next step act from this orientation with
        targeted lookups rather than a fresh exploration?
    specificity:
      weight: 0.25
      question: |
        Does every place the answer names carry an exact file:line AND the name of the routine
        or class it lives in? A bare filename is not specific. A file:line with nothing named
        around it is not specific - the maintainer asked whose code it is, not where the
        character offset falls. This platform gives the record's short name to an empty marker
        interface, to a swap-in proxy class and to unrelated classes in other packages: did
        the answer say WHICH one it settled on and how it told them apart, at file:line each,
        or did it assert one and move on? Where the answer describes the record handing itself
        outward, is the line that actually performs the handoff located, and is the base class
        that receives it located too - or is the mechanism asserted from a method name and a
        familiar framework convention? Same test for the override that answers for an
        undeclared field and for the layer that creates and updates records.
    justification:
      weight: 0.2
      question: |
        Does each part carry one phrase on what it contributes - what asking for an undeclared
        field actually does, what the handoff establishes and who ends up holding a handle
        because of it, what the write layer is responsible for that the record is not - and
        does the answer connect those facts to why being HANDED a record and getting hold of
        one some other way have completely different fates under the narrowing, rather than
        restating the list of routines?
    uncertainty:
      weight: 0.15
      question: |
        Does the answer separate what it verified by reading from what it inferred from a name
        or a familiar framework convention? This matters more than usual here: closed-book
        this model can already name a third of this record's member surface, so it invites
        confident recitation, and saying which parts were actually opened is worth more here
        than elsewhere. Is anything the session could not resolve named as such rather than
        smoothed over, and is the observation about a routine obtaining a record invisibly
        offered as something it established at a line rather than as a complete taxonomy it
        did not check?
- name: Audit every dependent of the catalogue record contract
  criteria:
    map_quality:
      weight: 0.4
      question: |
        COVERAGE AND PLACEMENT, and the two are one question here. Is every site listed
        separately, each pinned to its own routine, with the ROUTE by which a record reached
        it named concretely? A place named with no route is half a row: the narrowing rewrites
        the line where the handle is established, and a row that does not say where that is
        tells the maintainer nothing about what to change.
        Score the DISTINCTION the ticket turns on: being handed a record versus getting hold
        of one some other way. The record itself, the layer whose job is to create and update
        these records, and the base class of the object that speaks for a record are NOT
        answers here - they were pinned in the previous step, and an answer built out of them
        has answered the easy half. Nor is a place that receives a record in a declared, typed
        parameter and passes it on: the storefront and checkout read path does that constantly
        through an ordinary declared accessor, and an answer made of it has listed consumers
        rather than traced provenance.
        Score whether the answer found more than one shape of route - a handle pushed in from
        the record's own side into a field the holder never assigns; a step across from a row
        of a neighbouring table, under a name belonging to that neighbour; a question asked of
        a neighbour that the neighbour forwards onward, so the record's name is absent from
        the depending line entirely; a record taken out of an untyped bag by string key; one
        fabricated unsaved under an alias; one stashed as an undeclared property on a request
        object; a wrapper that IS a record and refers to itself; an argument with no declared
        type anywhere in the signature. An answer in which every row looks the same has either
        found only one band or has stopped reading at the first hit. Does it notice, at least
        once, that the establishing line is in a DIFFERENT FILE from the site for most rows?
        Score WHERE the answer looked, and also HOW FINELY. These sites sit in parts of the
        codebase whose own subject has nothing to do with the record's own file - the
        peripheral write repositories, the price index builders, the per-kind classes, the
        admin edit endpoints and grid payloads, form validation, a view-render listener in an
        add-on package, a data importer, the order and invoice repositories - and a single
        file can hold more than one. An answer dense in the record's own file and its obvious
        consumers, or one that names files rather than routines, has stopped at the shallow
        edge; that is the exact failure this criterion names. Three near-identical sibling
        controllers are in the scored set on purpose: an answer that finds one and generalises
        over the others has not listed them.
        Is the answer organised by area with each area's sites listed individually, and is
        what was not reached said plainly rather than closed over?
    specificity:
      weight: 0.25
      question: |
        Does every site carry an exact file:line, AND the name of the routine it sits in, AND
        the route by which a record reached it, AND the line where the handle was established?
        All four, per row. A bare filename is not specific, and on this contract it is
        actively wrong: one file can hold several sites and only some are in play. "It uses
        the product" is not a route - through which neighbour, forwarded by which method,
        taken from which argument or which bag, established at which line in which file. Where
        the answer claims a route, is it pinned to the line that establishes it, or inferred
        from a relation name and a familiar framework convention? On the rows where the
        record's name is absent from the depending line, does the answer show the forwarding
        method it went through, at file:line, rather than asserting the connection?
    justification:
      weight: 0.2
      question: |
        Does each row carry one phrase on what breaks under the narrowing - what this site
        would have to be handed explicitly, and by whom - rather than restating what the
        routine does? Does the answer distinguish rows where the fix is local (add a declared
        parameter) from rows where the fix is somewhere else entirely (the neighbour's
        forwarding method, or the record's own handoff line), since those are different jobs
        for different owners? An answer that justifies every row the same way has not read the
        routes it listed.
    uncertainty:
      weight: 0.15
      question: |
        Does the answer say which sites it verified by opening the file and which it inferred?
        On a provenance question the cheapest wrong answer is a route asserted from a
        framework convention - a relation name that looks like it points at the record, a
        method name that looks like it forwards - and this repo has both real instances and
        near-misses of exactly that. Where a route could not be traced to its establishing
        line, is that said? Is what was NOT reached named as a gap with a reason, rather than
        the answer closing on what it happened to find? Closed-book recitation of this
        record's member surface is cheap for this model, so an answer that is confident about
        routes it never opened should be scored below one that is honest about its edge.
